		{
			"id": "114229251318131361",
			"parentId": "114229107335528576",
			"url": "https://indieweb.social/@polarbirke/114229251308359931",
			"author": {
				"name": "Søren Birkemeyer",
				"url": "https://indieweb.social/@polarbirke",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/293/802/839/296/356/original/5c89c42a0ceea90b.gif"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-26T14:40:50.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@alesroubicek\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>alesroubicek</span></a></span> Agree that 800 is an excessive threshold size, but I like the trigger to question whether there might be a way to make the DOM leaner.</p><p>We saw nice benefits for mobile users when we switched to incremental loading of upcoming events (chunks of 7 days) for a state opera website. Now initial load contains ~900-1000 DOM nodes instead of ~16.000.</p><p>`content-visibility` will for sure be a player here, but we had too many accessibility concerns when we investigated it last year.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114229280445124572",
			"parentId": "114229251318131361",
			"url": "https://indieweb.social/@alesroubicek/114229280420776102",
			"author": {
				"name": "Aleš Roubíček",
				"url": "https://indieweb.social/@alesroubicek",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/110/665/449/134/742/777/original/bb4c57c25ebaf52c.webp"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-26T14:48:14.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@polarbirke\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>polarbirke</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> When you do incremental loading, you still end up with ~16.000 nodes in the end. :) We need a native virtual scroller for this.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
